v1 westbank-alerts — give the MiniMax path a success rate, and make its failure
legible. Apply from the service root:

    cd /opt/stacks/palestine/services/westbank-alerts
    cp -a app/llm/minimax_client.py app/llm/minimax_client.py.bak-$(date +%Y%m%dT%H%M%S)
    patch -p1 --dry-run < ~/palestine-v2/ops/patches/v1-minimax-metrics.patch
    patch -p1 < ~/palestine-v2/ops/patches/v1-minimax-metrics.patch
    docker compose up -d --build            # the container runs the baked code

WHAT IT CHANGES
  * per-call outcome counters, surfaced through the EXISTING `stats()` — which
    `GET /quality/checkpoints` already publishes as `llm`, so the number appears
    on a surface that exists rather than a new one.
  * `success_rate` is None when nothing has been attempted. Not 1.0, not 0.0.
  * a 200 with no `choices` says what the upstream actually returned instead of
    "'NoneType' object is not subscriptable", which is what all 29,686 failures
    said and why none of them pointed at billing.

NOT CHANGED: the fail-safe contract. `complete_json` still returns None on every
problem and the rules pipeline still runs. Nothing about ingestion moves.

WHY THIS IS A PATCH AND NOT AN EDIT
ops/minimax_alarm.py measures the same thing from OUTSIDE the container today,
from the journal and llm_cache, so the alarm does not wait for a deploy window.
This patch replaces that estimate with the real counter when Zaid rebuilds.
Applying it does not retire the alarm — the alarm watches the counter too.

--- a/app/llm/minimax_client.py	2026-09-19 17:20:19.501284382 +0000
+++ b/app/llm/minimax_client.py	2026-09-19 17:20:19.598283817 +0000
